+# Default Jobs Taxonomy
+
+Fyrnheim ships an opinionated default taxonomy that classifies dbt models by the **job** each model performs, not only by the folder or layer where it lives. Layers describe *where* a model lives; jobs describe *what* the model does. A single model can perform several jobs at once.
+
+The taxonomy is a starting point for conversation, not a universal truth. It is intentionally configurable and humble: every default can be overridden by a project-specific rules file passed to `fyr dbt classify`.
+
+## Why jobs, not only layers
+
+Layers (staging / intermediate / marts, or medallion bronze / silver / gold) are useful organizing structures, but they are not enough to describe model purpose. A staging model may perform source mapping, format alignment, and data contract enforcement all at once. Two models in the same `marts/` folder may serve very different jobs.
+
+Fyrnheim's default jobs make that explicit and inspectable, so teams and AI agents can discuss *why* a model exists in the graph.
+
+## Using the defaults
+
+`fyr dbt classify` uses the default taxonomy automatically when no `--rules` file is supplied:
+
+```bash
+fyr dbt scan --output .fyrnheim/dbt-inventory.json
+fyr dbt classify \
+  --inventory .fyrnheim/dbt-inventory.json \
+  --output .fyrnheim/dbt-classification.json
+```
+
+The default rules set `allow_multiple_labels: true` and `flag_layer_job_mismatch: true`, because the jobs framing expects a model to perform more than one job and to surface when a job looks out of place for its layer.
+
+## The default jobs
+
+Each job below has a description, signals, examples, anti-examples, suggested principles, and the layers it most often relates to. Suggested principles are not enforced here; M118 handles enforcement.
+
+### source_mapping
+
+Bring raw source data into the model graph with stable names, grain, and lightweight selection.
+
+- **Signals**: lives in staging or bronze; name begins with `stg_`; depends directly on a source; renames and selects without business logic.
+- **Examples**: `stg_customers` selecting and renaming columns from `raw.customers`; `bronze_orders` exposing raw orders with stable names.
+- **Anti-examples**: a staging model that joins two sources and applies business rules (that is `business_rule_application`); a `stg_orders` that aggregates to a monthly grain.
+- **Suggested principles**: keep 1:1 with a source; avoid joins and row-level logic; prefer explicit selection over `select *`.
+- **Related layers**: staging, bronze.
+
+### format_alignment
+
+Standardize data shapes so downstream models can trust types, casing, encoding, and units.
+
+- **Signals**: name contains `clean`, `cast`, `typed`, `normalized`, or `formatted`; intermediate/silver model that only adjusts formats.
+- **Examples**: `int_customers_cleaned` casting text ids to integers; `silver_events_normalized` converting timestamps to UTC.
+- **Anti-examples**: a model that changes grain or joins entities (that is `business_rule_application`); a staging model that only renames (that is `source_mapping`).
+- **Suggested principles**: do one format adjustment per column family; never change grain; make casts explicit and lossless.
+- **Related layers**: intermediate, silver.
+
+### data_contract_enforcement
+
+Assert and enforce the shape of data that crosses a boundary: not-null, uniqueness, accepted values, referential integrity, freshness.
+
+- **Signals**: several attached tests; tagged `contract`, `certified`, or `trusted`; sits at a layer boundary.
+- **Examples**: a silver model with not-null and unique tests on its primary key; a staging model tagged `contract` gating raw data.
+- **Anti-examples**: a model with a single sanity test (ordinary hygiene); a mart that merely exposes already-tested data.
+- **Suggested principles**: test the contract at the boundary; prefer explicit named contracts; make failures actionable and noisy.
+- **Related layers**: staging, silver, gold.
+
+### entity_definition
+
+Define a core business entity (customer, order, account) with a stable identity and descriptive attributes.
+
+- **Signals**: name or path references a single entity; tagged `entity`; lives under `models/intermediate/entities/`.
+- **Examples**: `int_customers_entity` resolving a stable `customer_id`; `dim_accounts` defining the account entity.
+- **Anti-examples**: a fact table of events (that is `analytical_output_shaping` or `business_rule_application`); a staging model that only renames (that is `source_mapping`).
+- **Suggested principles**: one entity per model; resolve identity once and reuse; separate entity definition from metrics.
+- **Related layers**: intermediate, silver.
+
+### business_rule_application
+
+Apply the joins, calculations, and logic that turn aligned data into meaningful records.
+
+- **Signals**: intermediate/silver model that joins multiple models; name begins with `int_`; applies calculations or enrichment.
+- **Examples**: `int_orders_with_customers` joining orders to customers; `silver_subscriptions_renewed` flagging renewals.
+- **Anti-examples**: a staging model that only selects and renames (that is `source_mapping`); a final mart shaped for a dashboard (that is `analytical_output_shaping`).
+- **Suggested principles**: name the rule the model applies; keep one major rule per model; document assumptions inline.
+- **Related layers**: intermediate, silver.
+
+### analytical_output_shaping
+
+Shape data for consumption by people, dashboards, or downstream systems.
+
+- **Signals**: lives in marts/gold; name begins with `fct_` or `dim_`; has downstream exposures; materialized as a table.
+- **Examples**: `fct_orders` exposing order events for analytics; `dim_customers` shaped for BI tooling.
+- **Anti-examples**: an intermediate model still applying logic (that is `business_rule_application`); a model with no consumers that is really an intermediate step.
+- **Suggested principles**: optimize grain and columns for the consumer; name outputs by the question they answer; keep marts thin.
+- **Related layers**: marts, gold.
+
+### quality_validation
+
+Validate data quality through dedicated test models, assertions, or QA models.
+
+- **Signals**: name contains `quality`, `assert`, `qa`, or `test`; materialized as `test`; tagged `quality` or `qa`.
+- **Examples**: `orders_quality_checks` failing when revenue is negative; `assert_customer_ids_unique` surfacing duplicates.
+- **Anti-examples**: a mart that happens to have tests (that is `analytical_output_shaping`); a staging model with ordinary hygiene tests (that is `source_mapping` with a contract).
+- **Suggested principles**: make validation models fail loudly; validate at the boundary; prefer reusable checks.
+- **Related layers**: staging, intermediate, marts.
+
+## Layers and mismatch detection
+
+The default taxonomy also describes the common dbt layers and the jobs each layer usually implies:
+
+| Layer | Detect | Implied jobs |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| staging | `/staging/` path, `staging` tag, `stg_` name | source_mapping, data_contract_enforcement |
+| bronze | `/bronze/` path, `bronze` tag | source_mapping |
+| intermediate | `/intermediate/` path, `intermediate` tag, `int_` name | format_alignment, entity_definition, business_rule_application |
+| silver | `/silver/` path, `silver` tag | format_alignment, data_contract_enforcement, entity_definition, business_rule_application |
+| marts | `/marts/` path, `mart` tag, `fct_`/`dim_` name | analytical_output_shaping, business_rule_application |
+| gold | `/gold/` path, `gold` tag | analytical_output_shaping |
+
+When `flag_layer_job_mismatch` is true (the default for the default taxonomy), Fyrnheim flags a model when it performs a job that is not implied by its detected layer. This is informational evidence, not an error: it surfaces places where a model's location and its behavior disagree, which is often where the most interesting modeling conversations happen.
+
+Each model record includes a `layer` assessment:
+
+```json
+{
+  "layer": {
+    "detected": "marts",
+    "implied_jobs": ["analytical_output_shaping", "business_rule_application"],
+    "mismatch": true,
+    "unexpected_jobs": ["source_mapping"],
+    "mismatch_evidence": [
+      {
+        "field": "layer",
+        "operator": "job_not_implied",
+        "expected": ["analytical_output_shaping", "business_rule_application"],
+        "actual": ["analytical_output_shaping", "source_mapping"],
+        "message": "model in layer 'marts' performs job(s) ['source_mapping'] not implied by that layer (...)"
+      }
+    ],
+    "evidence": [/* layer detection evidence */]
+  }
+}
+```
+
+## Overriding the defaults
+
+The default taxonomy is overridable. Provide a custom rules file to `fyr dbt classify --rules` to replace it entirely, or copy the default rules into your project and adjust them. See [dbt Model Classification](dbt-model-classification.md) for the rule schema.
+
+A project can also guide classification explicitly with `meta.job` on a model:
+
+```yaml
+models:
+  - name: int_orders
+    meta:
+      job: business_rule_application
+```
+
+The default rules check `meta.job` for every job, so explicit intent wins over heuristics.
+
+## Relationship to dbt layers
+
+Jobs do not replace layers. Layers remain a useful organizing convention; jobs add purpose. The two views compose: a model in `models/staging/` performing `source_mapping` is aligned; a model in `models/marts/` performing `source_mapping` is worth a conversation. Fyrnheim reports both so teams can decide.
